The parts the concurrent queues share.

- `queue_parts.hpp`: `queue_line`, the 128 bytes that keep indices written by different threads
  apart (x86 fetches cache lines in pairs); `queue_capacity` (a power of two, with a minimum),
  `queue_layout` / `queue_layout_of` (one block: a header of shared indices, then the slots at
  their alignment), and `queue_release`, which cleans up and destroys a left element.
- `mpmc_core.hpp`: Vyukov's bounded multi-producer multi-consumer ring as free functions over a
  borrowed `mpmc_ring` (counters, cells, mask), so `mpmc_queue` and `channel` keep their own
  block layouts. Each cell's sequence number says whose turn it is: equal to the position, a
  producer may claim it; position + 1, a consumer may. Claiming is a compare-exchange on the
  side's counter; publishing is a release store of the next sequence, which is what orders the
  element's bytes for the other side. Capacity is at least 2: with one cell, full and empty
  would carry the same sequence.

The channel's waits are event counts over `sys::futex_wait`: a sleeper counts itself, reads the
event word, tries the queue once more, then sleeps on that word; the other side, after moving an
element, fences and looks at the sleeper count, and only when it is not zero bumps the word and
wakes one. Either the sleeper's last try sees the element, or the waker sees the sleeper, so no
wake is lost, and an uncontended push or pop makes no syscall.
